The '''Sarta Colony of Guajicutea (1705–1723)''' covers the period from the end of the [[Sarta-Volisania War]] through the peaceful transfer of sovereignty from [[Sarta]] to the [[Republic of Guajicutea]] in 1723. The post-war colony experienced an influx of Sartan elites fleeing the devastated mainland, resumed Volisanian trade, and the growth of a wealthy Sartan-Guaji landowning class. The [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]] expanded the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] to support Volisania's [[First War of the Isthmus|war in Balboa]], becoming enormously wealthy. A republican movement, influenced by {{w|Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment}} ideas from southern [[Illypnia]] and the [[Commonwealth of Louyang]] in the [[Zong]] colony, culminated in a peaceful revolution: in 1723, Sarta ceded control to the [[Padres de Guajicutea]], who established a senate elected by landowners and a [[Cónsul]] as head of state.

The new republic was highly unequal: political power was concentrated in the hands of the Sartan-Guaji landowning elite, while Guaji natives, [[Kaftians]], and [[Bayani]] — who had lived on the island for generations — were excluded from political representation.

== Post-war conditions (1705–1710) ==

The Treaty of 1705 left the colony under Sartan sovereignty but stripped of its primary industrial asset. The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] was now operated by the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]], a Volisanian royal trading company. The Sartan naval squadron at [[Waribá]] — the only Sartan fleet to survive the war — remained stationed in the harbor, dependent on the Volisanian company for repair and maintenance.

[[Captain Andrés Caldera]] continued as military administrator. The colonial economy remained depressed. Sarta, bankrupted and landlocked, could provide no financial support. The colonial administration survived on trade with [[Mercia]], [[Breisland]], and [[Burawa]], and on the limited revenue the Companyia Reial generated through port activity at Waribá.

Sarta attempted to tax the colony to recoup its war losses, but the colonial population — already impoverished by years of crisis — had little to give. Conditions on Guajicutea were poor, but they were better than in mainland Sarta, where the war's devastation was total.

== Sartan elite influx and royal land sales ==

To recoup its war losses, the Sartan crown organized a program of selling royal lands in Guajicutea — lands held by the colonial administration — to Sartan elites. The program served two purposes: it raised funds for the bankrupt mainland, and it incentivized wealthy Sartans to emigrate to Guajicutea by offering them landholdings they could not acquire in the devastated mother country.

Beginning in the late 1690s and accelerating after 1705, Sartan elites, nobles, and wealthy families purchased these royal lands and relocated to Guajicutea. The colony, though poor, offered relative stability, arable land, and access to maritime trade that landlocked Sarta could no longer provide. The land sales program transformed the colony's demographic and economic profile: the influx increased the population and tax base, filling the colonial coffers with wealth transferred from Sarta, but also increased reliance on foreign imports, as the new arrivals brought consumption habits the local economy could not satisfy.

The landowning class that emerged from these sales — Sartan elites who had purchased royal estates, established trading houses, and integrated into the colonial administration — would form the political base of the republican movement. The program shifted the colony's political center of gravity: Guajicutea became more important to Sarta than any remaining mainland territory, a colony now wealthier and more functional than the mother country.

== First War of the Isthmus and the Companyia Reial ==

In 1711, a year after civilian rule was restored in Guajicutea, [[Volisania]] went to war with [[Zong]] in the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716), fought in the [[Balboa]] region. The Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran, which operated the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] and controlled port facilities at Waribá, expanded shipbuilding and port operations to support the Volisanian war effort.

The war created enormous demand for ships, naval stores, and repair facilities. The Astillero Real, which had sat idle during the Sarta-Volisania War, was now fully operational under Volisanian management, producing vessels for the Volisanian navy and merchant marine. The Companyia Reial invested heavily in expanding the dry docks, warehouses, and port infrastructure at Waribá. Port activity at Waribá surged, and the colonial economy benefited from the increased commerce.

Volisania lost the war in 1715. The loss opened trade routes to Zong territories — [[Zong]], [[Louyang]], and [[Sinchew]] — and goods from these regions flooded into the Capuyaquiran market. The influx of cheap Zong manufactured goods and raw materials drove down prices across the board. Cotton products — raw cotton and textiles — became cheaper to import from Zong than to produce locally, effectively killing Guajicutea's cotton industry.

=== Economic shift ===

The collapse of the cotton industry forced the colony to shift to other exports. [[Sugar]] became the primary replacement cash crop: it suited the tropical climate and the existing agricultural infrastructure, and commanded high prices in Volisanian, Breislandic, and Mercian markets. [[Tobacco]], already a pre-colonial export, expanded as a secondary cash crop. Tropical [[dye]]s — {{w|indigo}} and {{w|annatto}} — became valuable low-weight, high-value exports suited to smallholder production. Dried fish and cassava flour, already established exports, scaled to meet demand from the growing colonial population and foreign merchant ships calling at Waribá.

The colony's trade pattern shifted: raw materials and agricultural goods out, cheap manufactured goods from Zong in. The Sartan-Guaji landowning class invested in sugar processing and tobacco curing, while smallholder farmers — Guaji and Bayani — shifted to subsistence food production alongside cash crop farming. The economic transformation was uneven: landowners profited from the new cash crops, while laborers and smallholders bore the costs of transition.

== Republican movement ==

The colony's growing wealth and its political subordination to a bankrupt, landlocked rump state produced a growing sense of grievance among the Sartan-Guaji elite. The colony was more prosperous than the mother country, yet remained subordinate to it — paying taxes to a state that could provide nothing in return and losing sovereignty to a Volisanian company that controlled the colony's primary industrial asset.

=== Literature ===

Republican and liberal ideas, part of the broader [[Age of Enlightenment]] movement sweeping southern [[Illypnia]] — particularly in [[Mercia]] and [[Breisland]] — reached Guajicutea through trade networks and Sartan émigrés. These ideas argued for self-governance modeled after {{w|ancient republic|ancient republics}}, with representative institutions and elected leadership.

The most influential text was the "[[Discurso sobre la República Libre]]" (Discourse on the Free Republic), circulated among the Sartan-Guaji elite circa 1718. The work argued that Guajicutea should be self-governing, with a senate elected by property holders and a head of state chosen to represent the senate in foreign affairs. It drew on the example of the [[Commonwealth of Louyang]], the representative system established in the [[Zong]] colony of [[Louyang]], which gave landowners equal representation in the political process.

=== Don Vicente de la Vega ===

The republican movement was led by [[Don Vicente de la Vega]], a former Sartan nobleman who had emigrated to Guajicutea during the Sarta-Volisania War. De la Vega became a large landowner through the royal land sales and was exposed to Enlightenment literature through trade connections with Mercia and Breisland. He became convinced that Guajicutea's interests were no longer served by subordination to a landlocked, bankrupt rump state.

De la Vega's argument to the Sartan king was pragmatic: the colony was self-governing in practice, Sarta had no navy to enforce control, and the republican movement was growing. A peaceful transfer of sovereignty would preserve good relations and trade between Guajicutea and Sarta, while a refusal would risk a violent revolt that Sarta could not suppress. The king, facing these realities and influenced by Enlightenment ideas circulating in Sarta as well, agreed.

== Peaceful Revolution of 1723 ==

In 1723, Sarta formally ceded control of Guajicutea to the [[Padres de Guajicutea]] — the founding group of Sartan-Guaji landowners, merchants, and intellectuals who had organized the republican movement. The transfer was peaceful, negotiated through diplomatic channels rather than military action.

The Padres de Guajicutea established the [[Republic of Guajicutea]]. The new government consisted of a senate elected by landowners and a [[Cónsul]] as head of state, chosen to represent the senate in international affairs. The structure was modeled after ancient republics and the Louyang Commonwealth, with suffrage restricted to property holders.

The new republic was highly unequal. Political power was concentrated in the hands of the Sartan-Guaji landowning elite — the families that had purchased royal lands, built trading houses, and led the republican movement. Guaji natives, [[Kaftians]] descended from enslaved people brought during the Burawi colonial period, and [[Bayani]] migrants who had labored in the agricultural fields for generations were excluded from political representation. The republic was, at its founding, a government of the wealthy, by the wealthy, and for the wealthy.

